d. \( [ML^{-1}] \)
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The coefficient of friction is defined as the ratio of the frictional force to the maximum vertical force penetrating the surface.  
Frictional force \( F_f = \mu N \), where \( \mu \) is the coefficient of friction and \( N \) is the net force perpendicular to the surface (vertical force).  
\[
\mu = \frac{F_f}{N}
\]  
\[
[F_f] = [M L T^{-2}] \quad \text{(from } F = ma \text{, where } a = L T^{-2})
\]  
\[
[N] = [M L T^{-2}] \quad \text{(since } N = mg \text{, where } g = L T^{-2})
\]  
Thus, the coefficient of friction is:  
\[
[\mu] = \frac{[F_i}{[N]} = \frac{MT^{-2}}{MT^{-2}} = M^0 L^0 T^0
