The standard enthalpies of formation of CaO(s), H2O(l) and Ca(OH)2(s) are -635.1 kJ/mol, -285.8 kJ/mol, and -986.2 kJ/mol respectively.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔH° = Σ ΔH°f(products) - Σ ΔH°f(reactants)

where ΔH°f is the standard enthalpy of formation.

For the given reaction:

ΔH° = [ΔH°f(Ca(OH)2(s))] - [ΔH°f(CaO(s)) + ΔH°f(H2O(l))]

Now, we can plug in the given values:

ΔH° = [(-986.2 kJ/mol)] - [(-635.1 kJ/mol) + (-285.8 kJ/mol)]

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol - (-635.1 kJ/mol - 285.8 kJ/mol)

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol + 635.1 kJ/mol + 285.8 kJ/mol

ΔH° = -986.2 + 635.1 + 285.8

ΔH° = -65.3 kJ/mol

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction between calcium oxide and water to form calcium hydroxide is -65.3 kJ/mol.
