A's distance after 3 hours:
\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Rate} \times \text{Time} = 10 \text{ km/h} \times 3 \text{ h} = 30 \text{ km}
\]

When B stops, A is 30 km away from the starting point.

After B starts, both A and B are moving. The relative speed of B with respect to A is:
\[
\text{Relative speed} = 20 \text{ km/h} - 10 \text{ km/h} = 10 \text{ km/h}
\]

The time it takes for B to reach A is:
\[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Relative speed}} = \frac{30 \text{ km}}{10 \text{ km/h}} = 3 \text{ hours}
\]

The distance B must take to write A is:
\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Rate} \times \text{Time} = 20 \text{ km/h} \times 3 \text{ h} = 60 \text{ km}
\]

