","The maximum deformation of a triangular-shaped (triangle) cantilever beam with a concentrated end force \( P \) is given by:
\[
\delta_{\text{max}} = \frac{p L^3}{3 E I}
\]
where \( p \) is the length of the material. For a triangular plate, the cross-sectional length is \( p = \frac{b}{2} \). Substituting this into the equation:
\[
\Delta_{\text{max}} = \frac{P L^3}{3 E I}
\]
The cross-sectional moment of inertia \( I \) for a triangular plate is:
\[
I = \frac{b t^3}{36}
\]
Substituting \( I \) into the deformation equation:
\[
\Delta_{\text{max}} = \frac{P L^3}{3 E \left(\frac{b t^3}{36}\right)} = \frac{12 p L^3}{E b t^3}
\]
Substituting \( p = \frac{b}{2} \):
\[
\Delta_{\text{max}} = \frac{12 \left(\frac{b}{2}\right) L^3}{E b t^3} = \frac{6 L^3}{E t^3}
\]
Thus, the deformation is:
\[
\delta_{\text{max}} = \frac{6 p L^3}{E b t^3}
\]
Substituting \( p = \frac{b}{2} \):
\[
\delta_{\text{max}} = \frac{6 L^3}{E t^3}
\]

